Locator":"6042020","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"308ac12ec1c2452d820317ef15209a807939687b80973bdef5543516dd29af42","compact":"b40646c0c5b17d86a0c9f6dfe0f0f9f57e265cf0cf182299c1816384de8c45d6","nasal":"b40646c0c5b17d86a0c9f6dfe0f0f9f57e265cf0cf182299c1816384de8c45d6"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-42-21","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":42,"verse":21,"english":"Some, having their breasts pierced, lay on their sides. and some were riven with tridents; and the entrails of some had come out.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-52-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6042021","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3e3a9c4690067cacdb423e6b078a0d7ab97881384fdc341d79c5d2484d92716f","compact":"ddaf8a462ccd69a49471682e4466d9e66987919f15c67860af1674e8069e9b46","nasal":"11548e4f7930ba8b7517ac301ab30b3d0240a8ac52ed81541cdf140d679836ec"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-42-22","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":42,"verse":22,"english":"That mighty and dreadful encounter of the Räksasas and the monkeys, was waged with countless weapons and rocks and trees.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.69,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-52-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6042022","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d23b3062de54590646268713be9dd7681440d62b561cb5f3dbaa3b007619f363","compact":"b00897f8d05f6c8618afc15f78c030bcdb44f1a0737753547f1b4c3a860be71f","nasal":"0cf94856cfdc279b79dcf589620a4cada73f514f3ca277b0bdecbdefd5c92f2e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-42-23","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":42,"verse":23,"english":"That battle he come a musical entertainment' mellifluous with bow-string for Vina, having the neighing of chargers for its measure, and with the cries of Maindas' for its strains. 1. The reader has already perceived that the carrying out of a simile to a number of details is a characteristic as much of Vālmīki as of his rival in the epic sphere-Vyāsa, but the comparisons are never on all fours, as in the case before us. 2. A classical Hindu musical instrument, having metallic strings, with a pair of gourds at one end for the sounding-board. Vide Yantra-Kosa by Sir Saurindra Mohan Tagore, Mus-Doc. 3. A species of elephants.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.412,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-52-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6042023","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"49113d8e448d32eeeb77e36303d5e7e0e979273ae2893705189f80605afc8794","compact":"ad323cefaf53bc6841387a7f2aae6a29fedf0a8620dbf55f931850bcaa18a2dc","nasal":"8cac3ea1a978f96cd7fae53130979f11d7f432ae5610f62933305ad1c72d80c9"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-42-24","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":42,"verse":24,"english":"Then in the field, Dhūmrākṣa bow in hand, laughing, pursued the monkeys in all directions with showers of shafts.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.652,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-52-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6042024","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0d8d80ad40a5a77efa71a3d3d368a304d45733cb2b8239d9fcf542c8693f2ba6","compact":"d40b4de0e806554e5193f740c52a8b5f6cee89bbe1d9fe5ea1937b87b64d7d19","nasal":"d40b4de0e806554e5193f740c52a8b5f6cee89bbe1d9fe5ea1937b87b64d7d19"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-42-25","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":42,"verse":25,"english":"Seeing the forces hard beset by Dhūmrākşa and distressed thereat, the Windgod's offspring, waxing enraged, seizing a huge crag, approached (the Rākşasa).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.868,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-52-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sans